# Which glass has more water?
subtitle: "Water is poured from a short glass into a tall one: tap the glass with more, then hear why young children choose the tall one, as Piaget found."
tags: ["child development","piaget","conservation","cognitive psychology","visual perception"]
teaches: ["concepts/conservation","concepts/centration","developmental-psychology/cognitive-stages"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Aoede | a breezy quiz host; easy and warm","b":"gemini:Zephyr | a bright co-host with confident wrong hunches; natural, never exaggerated","take":"lines"}
format: quiz
thumb: "card orange"

## Which glass has more water?
affirm: playful
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: piaget
      title: La genèse du nombre chez l'enfant (The Child's Conception of Number)
      authors: J. Piaget, A. Szeminska
      year: 1941
      finding: in the liquid conservation task, children in the preoperational stage (about 2-7) judge that a taller, thinner glass holds more; most conserve by about 6-7

    path ga points [[230,487],[230,292],[470,292],[470,487]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path wa closed #2f6b8f points [[234,294],[466,294],[466,394],[234,394]] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    path gb points [[530,487],[530,292],[770,292],[770,487]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path wb closed #2f6b8f points [[534,294],[766,294],[766,394],[534,394]] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    path gc points [[840,640],[840,292],[940,292],[940,640]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path wc closed #2f6b8f points [[844,294],[936,294],[936,536],[844,536]] style.fill #2f6b8f style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.45 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    group glass_a members ["ga","wa"] hidden true
    group glass_b members ["gb","wb"] hidden true
    group glass_c members ["gc","wc"] hidden true
    arrow pour #2f6b8f from.x 690 from.y 521 to.x 820 to.y 615 hidden true
    box same "Same" x 870 y 470 width 150 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st_age "age 4: the tall one · age 7: the same" #2f6b8f cites ["piaget"] x 500 y 200 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st_pia "Jean Piaget, 1941" #2f6b8f cites ["piaget"] x 500 y 262 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Which glass has more water?"

A: Which glass has more water? Start with two identical glasses, filled to exactly the same level.
    draw glass_a glass_b

    draw pour

    erase wb

A: Now I pour this one into a tall, thin glass. Not a drop spilled.
    draw glass_c

    erase pour gb

    move glass_c by [-240,0] duration 1

B: Easy. The tall one, obviously. Look how high the water is.
    draw same

    ask "Which glass has more water now? Tap the glass, or tap Same." choose ["glass_a","glass_c","same"] answer same store c reveal.text "Same water!" reveal.at.x 830 reveal.at.y 600 reveal.size 36 reveal.keep true
        right: The same. Nothing was added or spilled; the water only changed shape.
        wrong: Think back to the pouring.

B: Hang on. I said the tall one.
    erase same

A: So do most four-year-olds. They point to the tall glass, because the water is higher.
    icon kid size 150 set twemoji x 850 y 420 icon_look picture of child
    arrow kid_arrow #b5482e from.x 770 from.y 430 to.x 715 to.y 430

B: Even after watching it being poured?
    highlight wc

A: Even then. The Swiss psychologist Jean Piaget made this little test famous.
    draw st_pia

A: He called it conservation: knowing that an amount stays the same when its shape changes. Most children get it at around six or seven.
    draw st_age

B: So that's why my little brother always grabbed the tall glass.
    highlight kid

A: Smart move, though: same water, bigger-looking prize.
    highlight glass_a glass_c
---
